WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.360 --> 00:00:06.160
What if a factory could adapt the moment conditions change?

2
00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:11.360
Manufacturing systems consist of interconnected machines, resources, and

3
00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:16.480
processes that harmonically transform raw materials into finished products.

4
00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:20.640
The performances of a manufacturing system are often pertinent to the rate

5
00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:25.199
of throughputs, the cost of production, the quality of finished products, the

6
00:00:25.199 --> 00:00:29.359
resilience to disruptions, or the life cycle sustainability.

7
00:00:29.359 --> 00:00:33.520
But real factories still face disruptions such as machine failures,

8
00:00:33.520 --> 00:00:38.719
material shortages, labor challenges, demand variation, and tooling problems.

9
00:00:38.719 --> 00:00:43.040
When a disruption occurs, production performance can degrade, and the system

10
00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:48.079
may miss target KPI. New technologies like digital twin have

11
00:00:48.079 --> 00:00:52.559
been increasingly deployed to address these challenges. A digital twin

12
00:00:52.559 --> 00:00:57.199
provides a digital representation of the manufacturing system for data streaming,

13
00:00:57.199 --> 00:01:02.719
visualization, simulation, process control, and optimization. Digital twin

14
00:01:02.719 --> 00:01:06.479
is powerful, but there is still a challenge in complex manufacturing

15
00:01:06.479 --> 00:01:10.560
systems. The number of possible configurations and disruption scenarios

16
00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:15.040
can grow rapidly. Traditional redesign and simulationbased search can become

17
00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:20.159
timeconuming and computationally expensive. This research presents a new

18
00:01:20.159 --> 00:01:24.880
generative AI framework for on-fly manufacturing system design under

19
00:01:24.880 --> 00:01:28.960
disruption. The goal is to generate adaptive system designs that align

20
00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:34.320
production performance with target KPI. The framework has three main components,

21
00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:39.360
a predictor, a generator, and a critic. Together, they learn relationships among

22
00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:45.119
machine availability, design decisions, and KPI outcomes. As the environment of

23
00:01:45.119 --> 00:01:50.159
a manufacturing job shop is dynamically evolving, plant managers are provided

24
00:01:50.159 --> 00:01:54.640
with this generative AI tool that generates candidate designs and

25
00:01:54.640 --> 00:02:00.000
implementation costs by taking a specific set of input variables. When

26
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:04.880
disruptions occur to machines, material handling systems, job orders, or raw

27
00:02:04.880 --> 00:02:09.360
materials, plant managers can feed new input variables to this tool and

28
00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:14.400
generate reconfigurable designs for evaluation and assessment. Similarly,

29
00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:18.879
when there is no disruption or the system is restored to conditions before

30
00:02:18.879 --> 00:02:24.720
disruptions, plant managers can also input a different set of KPI targets or

31
00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:28.800
job order variations to generate candidate designs for continuous

32
00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:33.519
improvements. In this vision, the physical factory, digital twin, and

33
00:02:33.519 --> 00:02:38.080
generative AI work together. The factory provides the real manufacturing

34
00:02:38.080 --> 00:02:42.160
environment. The digital twin provides simulation intelligence and the

35
00:02:42.160 --> 00:02:47.040
generative AI framework supports redesign decisions. This work shows how

36
00:02:47.040 --> 00:02:51.360
generative AI can support resilient manufacturing by helping factories

37
00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:57.400
respond faster to disruptions and changing performance goals.

